        <div id="blade-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 6-1/2-Inch vs 7-1/4-Inch: Choosing a Blade Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blade size decides depth of cut, and depth of cut decides what the saw can do. A 7-1/4-inch blade cuts roughly 2-7/16 to 2-5/8 inches deep at 90 degrees, which is enough to go through standard 2x lumber, actually 1-1/2 inches thick, with room to spare. More importantly, it still gets through that lumber at a 45-degree bevel. That is the reason 7-1/4 inches became the standard size for framing and deck work.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 6-1/2-inch blade cuts about 2-1/4 inches deep at 90 degrees and around 1-5/8 inches at 45 degrees. That is enough to cross-cut a 2x4 square, and plenty for plywood, OSB and trim, but a 45-degree bevel through 2x lumber is marginal, and you may need to finish the cut from the other side. In exchange, the smaller saw is lighter, easier to control one-handed and draws less current, so it cuts more per charge on the same battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most homeowners the 7-1/4-inch saw is the safer choice because it does everything, including the occasional bevel through a stair stringer or a rafter tail. Blades in this size are also far easier to find, in more tooth counts and specialty types, at any hardware store. If the saw will mainly break down sheet goods and cut trim, siding and fence boards, a 6-1/2-inch saw is lighter and entirely adequate.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is another reason 7-1/4-inch cordless saws now make sense even for occasional DIY: brushless motors and larger batteries have closed most of the power gap. A few years ago a cordless full-size saw could struggle badly in wet pressure-treated lumber. Today's brushless models on a 6.0Ah or larger battery cut through it steadily, though still not quite as relentlessly as a good corded saw on a long rip.
          </p>

