        <div id="blade-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Left Blade or Right Blade
          </h2>

          <p>
            Circular saws put the blade on one side of the motor, and which side matters more than most buyers realise. It decides whether you can see the cut line from your natural working position, which way the saw's weight falls, and whether sawdust blows toward you or away. There is no universally correct answer; it depends on which hand you use and how you like to work.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a right-blade saw, the common layout for sidewinders, a right-handed user has the motor between their eyes and the blade, so they either lean over to watch the line or follow the guide notch on the front of the shoe. On a left-blade saw, a right-handed user sees the blade and the line directly without leaning, which many people find more accurate for freehand cuts. Left-handed users experience the opposite.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rear-handle saws usually put the blade on the left, which is part of why framers who learned on worm-drive saws are so attached to them: a sight line straight down the blade, with the bulk of the saw on the supported side of the board. Blade side varies between models even within one brand. Milwaukee's current M18 FUEL 7-1/4-inch sidewinder, the 2834-20, is listed with the blade on the right, while its rear-handle saws keep the blade on the left.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our advice is simple. If you already use a circular saw and like it, buy one with the blade on the same side, because retraining your eye and your stance takes longer than you would expect. If you are new to circular saws and right-handed, try a left-blade saw in a store if you can. Whichever you choose, check the listing or the maker's specifications, because blade side is rarely obvious from product photos.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rear-handle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sidewinder or Rear-Handle (Worm-Drive Style)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most circular saws are sidewinders: the motor sits beside the blade and drives it directly, the handle is on top, and the saw is compact and relatively light. Many framers, particularly on the West Coast, have long preferred worm-drive saws, where the motor sits behind the blade and drives it through gears, with the handle at the back. Cordless rear-handle saws borrow that layout for battery power.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rear-handle layout has real advantages on long cuts. The saw is longer, which helps it track straight along a line; the handle behind the blade gives a natural pushing position; and with the blade on the left, a right-handed user sees the cut clearly. It also keeps your hand further from the blade. The downsides are weight and bulk. Milwaukee's M18 FUEL rear-handle saw, the 2930-20, weighs around 10 pounds before the battery goes on, and the layout is awkward for cutting overhead or one-handed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most cordless rear-handle saws are not true worm-drive designs, so their gearing and feel differ from a corded worm-drive even when the layout matches. For practical purposes, what you are buying is the ergonomics and the sight line. The Skil PWRCORE 20 rear-handle saw in our picks runs on two 20V batteries at once, giving 40V of power to drive the longer, heavier saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most homeowners and DIYers, a sidewinder is the better choice: lighter, easier to manoeuvre, easier to store and usually cheaper. Choose a rear-handle saw if you already learned on one, if you frame or cut decking in volume, or if you simply find it more controllable in your hands. It is a genuine preference, not an upgrade, and neither layout cuts better than the other.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Battery Size and Cuts Per Charge
          </h2>

          <p>
            The circular saw is the most power-hungry tool most people run on batteries, and it exposes a small battery immediately. A 7-1/4-inch brushless saw on a compact 2.0Ah pack will cut, but it bogs down in thick or wet lumber, trips its overload protection and runs flat quickly. On a 5.0Ah or larger high-output pack, the same saw can feel like a different tool entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturers publish cuts-per-charge figures, and they are useful within limits. Milwaukee quotes up to 750 cuts per charge for its 2834-20 with its large HD12.0 battery. Makita quotes up to 610 crosscuts in 2x4 for the XSH06 with two 5.0Ah packs. Skil quotes up to 600 for its rear-handle saw with two 5.0Ah batteries, and Ryobi more than 325 for the PBLCS300. Each figure uses a different battery and a different test, so do not rank saws by them.
          </p>

          <p>
            What those figures do tell you is the scale. One charge can cover a day of intermittent cutting on a deck or a remodel, but only with a suitable battery. As a practical rule, run a 7-1/4-inch saw on at least a 5.0Ah pack, preferably a high-output one, and keep a second battery on the charger. A 6-1/2-inch saw is more tolerant of a mid-size pack.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is also where platforms differ. DeWalt's FlexVolt batteries switch between 20V and 60V and power both the 20V DCS573 and the 60V DCS578. Makita's XSH06 takes two 18V packs at once, and its 40V XGT saws use a separate battery system. Ryobi has separate 18V and 40V lines, and our <a href="/ryobi-18v-vs-40v/">Ryobi 18V vs 40V</a> guide explains why those batteries do not cross over. For platform-level comparisons, see <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> and <a href="/dewalt-vs-makita/">DeWalt vs Makita</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">BATTERY RULES OF THUMB</p>
