        <div id="bevel-depth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Bevel Capacity and Depth of Cut
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every circular saw bevels to 45 degrees, the angle you need for mitred corners and most roof and stair work. What differs is how far past 45 degrees it goes, and how easy it is to set accurately. Most saws here bevel to somewhere between 50 and 57 degrees. The DeWalt DCS573 and DCS578 and the Milwaukee 2834-20 reach 57 degrees; the Makita XSH06 and Ryobi PBLCS300 reach 56; the Skil rear-handle saw reaches 53.
          </p>

          <p>
            Going beyond 45 degrees matters for specific jobs: steep roof pitches, some stair and rafter cuts, and outside corners that are not quite square. If you do not know whether you need it, you probably do not, but it costs nothing extra on most of these saws. More useful day to day are positive stops at 22.5 and 45 degrees, which let you set common angles quickly and repeatably without reaching for a protractor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Depth of cut at 45 degrees is the number to check if you cut bevels in 2x lumber. A 7-1/4-inch saw that cuts 2 inches deep at 45 degrees goes through 1-1/2-inch-thick lumber cleanly, with some allowance for blade wear; the DCS573 is rated at exactly that. A 6-1/2-inch saw at around 1-5/8 inches is borderline, and you will likely need to finish some bevel cuts from the other side of the board.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set the cut depth for every cut rather than leaving the saw at full depth. The blade should project about a quarter inch below the material. A shallow setting reduces the risk of kickback, gives a cleaner cut with less tear-out, and puts less strain on the battery, which translates directly into more cuts per charge. It takes a few seconds and is the single easiest improvement to how any saw performs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Electric Brakes, Guards and Kickback
          </h2>

          <p>
            An electric brake stops the blade within a couple of seconds of releasing the trigger instead of letting it coast to a stop. It is the one safety feature we would insist on, and most of the saws in our picks have one. Without a brake, a coasting blade is a common way people get hurt setting a saw down, and it slows you down too, because you wait for the blade to stop before moving on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kickback is the main hazard with any circular saw. It happens when the blade binds in the cut, usually because the offcut sags and pinches the blade or the saw drifts off line, and the saw jumps back toward the user. Support the work so the offcut falls free, keep the blade depth shallow, stand to one side of the cut line, and never reach under the material while the blade is turning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brushless saws add a layer of electronic protection. When the blade binds hard, many will cut power to protect the motor and battery. That is useful, but it is not a replacement for good technique, and it will not stop a kickback that has already started. Keep both hands on the saw wherever the design allows, and let the blade reach full speed before it touches the wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the lower guard every time you pick the saw up. It should snap closed on its own. Sawdust and pitch build-up can make it sluggish, especially after cutting pressure-treated lumber, and a guard that sticks open is dangerous. A quick clean with a brush and a dry lubricant keeps it moving freely, and it is worth doing at the end of every project.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NON-NEGOTIABLE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy a saw with an electric brake.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It stops the blade within a couple of seconds instead of letting it coast. Most of our picks have one, and if a budget saw does not, that alone is a reason to spend a little more.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="blades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. The Blade Matters More Than the Saw
          </h2>

          <p>
            The blade supplied with a cordless circular saw is usually a general-purpose 24-tooth carbide blade. It is fine for framing lumber, and it is poor for plywood, where it tears out the face veneer. Changing the blade does more for cut quality than switching between any two saws on this list, and it costs a fraction of the price.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cordless saws, choose thin-kerf blades. A thinner blade removes less material, which means less work for the motor and more cuts per charge, and many blade makers now sell lines designed specifically for battery saws. For framing, a 24-tooth thin-kerf blade is the standard. For plywood and sheet goods, a 40- to 60-tooth blade gives a far cleaner edge with much less splintering.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replace blades when they go dull rather than pushing harder. A dull blade burns the wood, needs more force to feed and drains the battery faster, which makes a good saw feel weak. Pressure-treated lumber and OSB dull blades quickly, so on a large deck project expect to change blades at least once.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need consistently straight, square rip cuts in sheet goods, a circular saw with a straightedge guide will get you close, but a table saw is the right tool for that job. Our <a href="/best-table-saw/">best table saws</a> guide covers jobsite and benchtop models. Many people end up owning both, using the circular saw to break full sheets down to a manageable size and the table saw for the finished rips.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Match the Saw to Your Battery Platform
          </h2>

          <p>
            As with every cordless tool, the battery you already own should guide your choice. The saws here come from five different battery systems, and none of them share batteries. A circular saw is also usually the tool that forces people to buy their first large battery, so it is a good moment to think carefully about which platform you are committing to for the long term.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already have an impact driver or a drill on one of these platforms, the saw from that platform is the right choice unless it lacks something you specifically need. If you are starting now, the saw is a good tool to build the platform around, since it needs the biggest batteries. Our <a href="/best-impact-driver/">best impact driver</a> guide covers the other tool most people buy alongside a saw for deck and fence work.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt and Milwaukee are the strongest choices for heavy, regular cutting, with deep saw lineups and high-output batteries. Makita offers excellent saws in both its 18V LXT and 40V XGT lines, and the dual-battery XSH06 lets existing LXT owners get serious power without a new battery system. Ryobi and Skil are the value options, and both are perfectly sound for homeowners who cut occasionally.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever platform you pick, include the battery when you compare prices. A bare saw that looks cheap can cost considerably more once you add a 6.0Ah pack and a charger, and a kit that includes them is often better value for a first saw. The saw you can afford to run on the right battery is a better buy than a more powerful one you run on the wrong one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the saw on a compact battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2.0Ah pack that is perfect for a drill will leave a 7-1/4-inch saw stalling in thick lumber and running flat after a handful of rips. Budget for at least a 5.0Ah pack, ideally a high-output one, when you buy the saw. The saw is only as capable as the battery you put on it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting plywood with the stock framing blade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 24-tooth blade supplied with most saws tears out plywood face veneers badly. A 40- to 60-tooth thin-kerf blade costs little and transforms cut quality on sheet goods. Keep the coarse blade for framing lumber and switch before cutting anything whose edge will show in the finished work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the blade at full depth</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running the blade at full depth for every cut increases kickback risk, tear-out and battery drain. Set the blade to project about a quarter inch below the material. It takes a few seconds per setup and gives cleaner, safer cuts and noticeably more cuts per charge from the same battery.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 6-1/2-inch saw for framing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 6-1/2-inch saw cross-cuts 2x lumber at 90 degrees but struggles to bevel through it at 45. If you plan to build decks, stairs or anything structural, the 7-1/4-inch size saves a lot of frustration. Choose the smaller saw only if sheet goods, trim and fence boards are most of your work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting between two supports</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the board is supported on both sides with the cut line in the middle, the two halves sag toward the blade, the kerf closes and the saw kicks back. Support the work so the offcut can fall away freely, or lay it fully on a sheet of rigid foam and cut through into that instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cordless circular saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, it is the 7-1/4-inch brushless saw from the battery platform they already own. If you are starting fresh, the DeWalt DCS573 is our top pick for its balance of power, weight and features. The Milwaukee M18 FUEL 2834-20 and the Makita XSH06 are equally strong choices for owners of those batteries, and the Ryobi PBLCS300 is the budget pick.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cordless circular saw as powerful as a corded one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Close, with the right battery. Current brushless 7-1/4-inch saws on large high-output packs cut 2x lumber and plywood about as fast as a typical corded saw. They still fall behind on long rips through wet or dense lumber, where a corded saw never tires and never needs a battery swap. For most home projects, the difference is small.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size battery do I need for a cordless circular saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 5.0Ah for a 7-1/4-inch saw, and preferably a high-output pack. Smaller batteries will run the saw, but it bogs down in thick material and needs recharging often. A 6-1/2-inch saw can manage on a mid-size pack. Owning two batteries, so one is always charging, is the practical minimum for a full day of cutting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I get a 6-1/2 or 7-1/4 inch circular saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Get 7-1/4 inches if you will bevel-cut 2x lumber, build decks or do any framing. Get 6-1/2 inches if you mainly cut plywood, trim and fence boards and want a lighter saw. The larger size is more versatile and uses the most widely available blades, which is why it is our default recommendation for a first saw.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a rear-handle circular saw better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not better, just different. Rear-handle saws track straighter on long cuts and give right-handed users a clear view of a left-side blade, which is why framers like them. They are also heavier and bulkier. For general DIY use, a sidewinder is easier to handle and store. Buy a rear-handle saw if you frame in volume or already prefer the layout.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an electric brake on a circular saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We would not buy a saw without one. An electric brake stops the blade within a couple of seconds of releasing the trigger, which prevents injuries from a coasting blade and saves time between cuts. Most brushless saws from the major brands include one, so choosing a saw with a brake rarely costs anything extra.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a cordless circular saw replace a table saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For breaking down sheet goods and rough cuts, yes, especially with a straightedge guide. For repeated, precise rip cuts, no; a table saw is faster, more accurate and safer for that work. Many DIYers use a circular saw to cut full sheets down to a manageable size and then a table saw for the finished dimensions.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

